# ── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# LF everywhere: in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# The repo was already 1801-to-3 LF; the three exceptions were written by a
# tool that GUESSED the ending from the file's existing bytes.  That guess is
# also how 78 working-tree files ended up with mixed endings, and how one file
# produced a 4133-line phantom diff for a 93-line change -- git's autocrlf
# clean filter declines to normalise a file whose endings are already mixed, so
# the damage compounds silently instead of being corrected on the next commit.
#
# Checking out LF (rather than the Windows default of "LF in the repo, CRLF in
# the working tree") takes the filter out of the loop entirely: the file on
# disk is byte-identical to the blob, so a diff can no longer lie, and any tool
# that reads or rewrites a file directly sees one convention.  MSVC, clang,
# cmake, ninja and Visual Studio all accept LF on Windows.
#
# This overrides core.autocrlf, which Git for Windows sets to `true` system-wide
# -- attributes win, so a fresh clone on any machine lands on LF regardless of
# the developer's global config.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the one consumer that genuinely wants CRLF: a batch file with
# LF-only endings can mis-parse labels and `goto` targets.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never rewritten, never diffed as text.  Git's NUL heuristic already catches
# most of these; naming them keeps a stray text-looking asset out of the sweep.
*.png  -text
*.jpg  -text
*.jpeg -text
*.gif  -text
*.bmp  -text
*.tga  -text
*.dds  -text
*.ktx  -text
*.hdr  -text
*.ico  -text
*.ttf  -text
*.otf  -text
*.woff -text
*.woff2 -text
*.wav  -text
*.mp3  -text
*.ogg  -text
*.m4a  -text
*.mp4  -text
*.webm -text
*.glb  -text
*.bin  -text
*.pak  -text
*.jirc -text
*.zip  -text
*.7z   -text
*.gz   -text
*.jar  -text
*.exe  -text
*.dll  -text
*.lib  -text
*.pdb  -text
*.so   -text
*.dylib -text
*.a    -text
*.wasm -text

# Vendored third-party sources — excluded from GitHub language statistics
**/third_party/** linguist-vendored=true
**/internal/** linguist-vendored=true
